  "author": "annambhatta",
  "attributionNote": "Annaṃbhaṭṭa, a South Indian author of the seventeenth century of the nyāya-vaiśeṣika school, wrote the aphorisms as an introduction for beginners and then commented on them himself in the Dīpikā; the two are printed together in most editions and the work became the standard first reader of the school. GRETIL's e-text carries the aphorisms, in the numbering of the edition.",
  "description": "The Tarkasaṃgraha sets out the seven categories of the nyāya-vaiśeṣika school — substance, quality, action, the universal, the particular, inherence and absence — with their divisions, and then the four means of knowledge: perception, inference with its members and its fallacies, comparison and verbal testimony, and the objects of knowledge, the self, the body, the senses, and the erroneous knowledge that binds. Its aphorisms are short enough to be learned by heart and exact enough to be argued over, and they are still the first text put into the hands of a student of Indian logic.",
  "historicalContext": "Written for the schools of Andhra and the Tamil country and carried through the Indian college curricula of the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, the Tarkasaṃgraha is the work through which Indian logic and epistemology were taught in modern India, and its categories were the terms in which the tradition explained itself to the new universities.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete as GRETIL publishes it: the whole compendium of 81 aphorisms, streamed from GRETIL at read time and indexed in reading order, since the work has no chapter division of its own. The library indexes each aphorism by the number the edition prints. This is the original Sanskrit, not a translation.",
